#### Contraction on the active sparse series

The release 005 census counts 406,253 assigned lexical coordinates and 22,083,067 open coordinates over 401,595 lexical records. A separate sentence-construction record is included in the 401,596-record replay. The 280,661 explicit zeros are assignments. Each remaining open domain contains all three signs. The four successors of the first sparse release resolved 100, 20, 115, and 171 cells. Together they removed 643.49 bits of marginal domain potential, or 0.00184% of the residual at release 001. GOOD and SOMETHING contain 87.94% of existing assignments. Twenty-five lexical banks have no assigned cells, including WORDS on the exact linked sense of *word*. [Initial sparse release](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-12-ssl-continuation/sparse-release-001-activation.json) · [Release 005](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-12-ssl-anatomical-tissue/sparse-release-005-activation.json)

The tissue round also permits an exact support calculation in its reviewed scope. Before the new rule, each compatible representation of the other banks admitted all three BODY signs on each of the 171 changed senses. The only applicable prior relation involving BODY connected it to already established SOMETHING=+1. The new rule connects it to already established PART=+1 and leaves one BODY sign per representation. Factoring the recorded constraints across these exact senses gives a support reduction of $3^{171}$. This is an exponential factor in the number of resolved cells. Geometric decay of residual domain potential across epochs calls for a separate measurement. The Lean theorem assumes fractional progress at each step. The equal-budget duration ablation shows one route to that progress, with 20 WANT values jointly enabled by two rules. [Tissue scope](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-12-ssl-anatomical-tissue/SOURCE_SCOPE.md) · [Decay theorem](proofs/resolution_greedy_decay.lean) · [Duration ablation](../../../../gse/data/lexicon_builder/2026-11-12/ssl-epoch-comparison/comparison.json)

