#### SSL checkpoint handoff: full-basis specification and experiment accounting

The November 23 continuation explicitly places the usable checkpoint inside SSL's epistemology. The object being learned is a reusable semantic specification. Its application constrains the population's admissible representations; independently supplied facts contribute their direct resolutions and the additional consequences they enable. Newly learned rules can also change the reach of later rules. The checkpoint is a frozen measurement boundary in this process, followed by continued propagation, typed surprise, refinement and residual teaching. [Verbatim request and claim ledger](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-and-legacy-hypotheses.md#ssl-checkpoint-handoff-and-epistemic-accounting)

The next foundation pass now has an explicit review ledger covering all 56 lexical coordinates and all 65 NSM units. It retains exact definitions, coordinate correspondence, currently supported own-bank domains, the applicable representation scale and existing constraint IDs. Fourteen scalar correspondences already have the expected own-coordinate singleton, forty-four remain open, and seven require construction or hierarchy review. These counts describe the linked coordinate claims, not complete prime signatures. The ledger writes no assignments. Source meanings or justified semantic derivations should discharge what they settle; unresolved GSE adaptations remain separately identified work. [Basis-wide ledger](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-foundation-review.json)

This handoff also makes a measurement blind spot explicit. The current epoch selector uses newly assigned singleton cells and singleton bridge effects as its reach proxy. A joint constraint can eliminate complete assignments without narrowing any marginal domain, so that proxy captures only part of the intended representation-space contraction. Exact supported-component measurements, when complete, must accompany it. Marginal domain potential remains separately named, and an unavailable joint count is not a zero gain. The frozen 022 run and its recorded selection method remain unchanged.

A scoped ablation makes that distinction concrete. For the stored dog definition's I, YOU and SOMEONE domains, the Cartesian three-bank subsystem has 27 combinations. Restoring its two already-admitted category relations leaves seventeen supported combinations, while all three marginal domains remain {-1,0,+1}. This is 0.6674 bits of joint log-support reduction with zero singleton gain and zero marginal-potential reduction. It is information already present in the specification, not newly learned progress or a count of the full 56-bank space. [Exact subsystem witness](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-joint-information-witness.json)

Workflow version 1.7 records this foundation/checkpoint pass and its epistemic accounting. Existing scholarship whose projection was missing is supplied source information; authoring a representation convention is a specification decision; independently audited reusable generalization is rule learning. Their propagated effects, model calls and actual review effort remain separately recorded. New diagnostics inform the next curriculum and become regression controls; fresh identities and cases are required for a subsequent independent test. Historical phases retain their original cohorts, budgets and units. [Updated workflow, §§3 and 12](../LLM_WORKFLOW/SEMANTIC_CONSTRUCTION_WORKFLOW.md)

The experiment targets SSL's structural amplification: additional uncertainty removed per unit of supplied specification, including the effect of learned rule bridges. Its bulk-exponential prediction is assessed on a sequence of versioned lexicon measurements with the stated contraction hypotheses. The theoretical mechanism is retained while the artifact's actual amplification, joint contraction, semantic correctness and runtime availability remain distinguishable empirical results. A high count of unassigned cells does not by itself measure all remaining joint uncertainty. [SSL dynamics, §2.5](01_PAPER_SKELETON.md)

The checkpoint execution scope is now explicit: discharge the full-basis source/projection review, apply warranted population rules to closure, target frequent meanings, and verify that their values reach ordinary GSE consumers before claiming a broadly usable checkpoint. The sparse-release gate alone permits too much abstention to establish that additional claim. A first source-projection review traces two concrete omissions: the exact curated I/YOU observations already carry first-/second-person features, while their own I/YOU bank domains remain open. Under the approved reference-only bank meanings, their own positive components have recorded derivations without model calls. The warrants have not been applied or counted as learned progress. One earlier model assertion on I and four on YOU require dependency review during integration. No other coordinate or same-spelling sense receives an assignment from these two warrants. The frozen 022 run continues unchanged. [Execution scope](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-and-legacy-hypotheses.md#checkpoint-execution-scope) · [Exact source witnesses and projection derivations](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-source-projection-warrants.json)

### Checkpoint implementation: source proofs and consumer scope

The checkpoint pass now has executable corrections at both sides of the lexicon interface. The registered closed-class adapter derives I=+1 and YOU=+1 for the two reviewed native pronoun definitions under the approved reference-only bank meanings. Other coordinates remain open unless independently justified. Full source witnesses survive unchanged; replay reconstructs each derived reference sign and rejects an unwarranted sign even when the source has nominal competence to write that bank. This is the integration of already available source information, not newly learned semantic structure.

The public sentence path also now distinguishes an asserted report from its reported content. In “Alice says that Bob left,” the reporting event is committed and the departure is attributed but uncommitted. Negating the report leaves the departure unknown; negating its content retains the content's own sign without asserting it. KNOW retains its previously approved commitment behavior. These checks run through the encoder, document intake, shared propositions, knowledge base and document-resolved narrative graph. They repair a consumer scope defect; they are not lexicon propagation gains.

The implementation supports atomic retirement of several dependent model suffixes with one full preflight replay. It coalesces overlapping roots on the same definition, preserves the complete original receipts, and rolls back every entry if any member fails. A real source-transition control retires the old synthetic BODY rulings, adds the source reference signs and verifies that BODY reopens to {-1,0,+1}. The focused receipts record 392 initial integration tests, 283 proof/retirement tests and two transition controls. These overlapping suites are separate verification scopes, not independent semantic samples. [Implementation and claim ledger](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-and-legacy-hypotheses.md#checkpoint-implementation-continuation)

The completed 022 arm records 118 new cells from two admitted rules and eight typed questions, with 401,596 definitions compared and zero reopenings. Its publication finalizer stopped because the formal design source changed after the original pins were recorded; a package-wide namespace change was also identified. Its historical pins remain intact. A new owned transition uses one working clone to retire the five affected real I/YOU model receipts, integrate the exact native proofs and replay the complete corpus under the current implementation. The transition's computed receipt, rather than this prose, states which phases have finished. It performs no activation. [Frozen arm result](../../../../gse/data/lexicon_builder/2026-11-12/ssl-explicit-genera/coherence-022/zipf/arm-result.json) · [Live transition receipt](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-reference-migration.json)

This phase has not yet established the requested broadly usable checkpoint. Full-basis warrant integration, broader ordinary-sense grounding and the practical common-language gate remain obligations. Its additional contribution to the SSL experiment is a clearer accounting boundary: source information supplied, approved representation decisions, independently learned rules, propagated consequences and runtime integration effects remain separately attributable. A successful sparse replay alone does not discharge those remaining checkpoint obligations.

### Method correction: identity eligibility and historical Zipf attribution

A subsequent audit located a curriculum gap in earlier persistent epochs, not only in the checkpoint investigation. The original epoch comparison selected are.n.01, the area unit, with the aggregate spelling count 265,672. Later recorded selections include will.v.01 (“decree or ordain”) with 108,306 and privation.n.01 under want (“a state of extreme poverty”) with 140,718. These are exact definition identities, but their priority counts were not attributed to those senses. The audit retains all 32 located teacher-selection records with their original hashes; earlier manual batches and the exact onset remain outside its verified scope. [Historical selection audit](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-curriculum-history-audit.json)

This affects the interpretation of the earlier Zipf arm: it ranked aggregate spellings within scoped families, which does not establish a curriculum of the most common meanings. Its observed assignments and replay counts remain measurements of that implemented method. The current singleton-gain and limited-bridge selection objective also omits joint-support reductions that leave marginal domains unchanged. Those historical comparisons therefore do not establish that the complete intended SSL controller was exercised. A corrected, separately versioned equal-budget comparison is required. The audit has not quantified the share of total runtime caused by these gaps.

The correction belongs to the shared constraint architecture. Context and type determine eligible sense identities before selection; frequency evidence must retain its own identity and scope; unknown sense frequency is not known zero. Significance then ranks lawful unresolved questions and propagates the consequences of accepted specifications. A public adversarial case also exposes a consumer gap: “The chemical symbol He denotes helium” is forced into the pronoun reading, although ordinary pronoun controls select the proper pronoun identity. This requires contextual identity constraints to reach grammatical selection. Reweighting frequency alone cannot supply that missing constraint application. [Scope, evidence and correction ledger](../../../../gse/docs/readings/2026-11-13-ssl-explicit-genera/checkpoint-and-legacy-hypotheses.md#historical-curriculum-attribution-correction)

### Mandatory joint-space phase — 2026-11-23

The founder explicitly required that the original coupled-constraint construction design govern the build, rather than treating each unresolved bank as an independent filling task. The formal build now carries the normative `gse.ssl-construction/2` contract, SSL-01 through SSL-11: reading eligibility before selection; complete applicable constraint closure; learning scoped exclusions and conjunctions; dynamic question selection; typed exception refinement; exact joint-space accounting where supported; whole-corpus frequency buckets; and executable anti-regression obligations. The historical coverage measurements remain intact. They do not retrospectively establish that the complete intended learning controller was exercised. [Formal execution contract](../../../../gse/docs/program/LEXICON_CONSTRUCTION_DESIGN.md#mandatory-ssl-execution-and-measurement-contract)

For this phase, define $N_t(w)=|\Omega_t(w)|$ as the number of complete 56-bank assignments satisfying the actual admitted constraints for definition $w$. Report $\sigma_{GSE,t}(w)=\log_2 N_t(w)$ and the corpus sum $\sum_w\sigma_{GSE,t}(w)$. This is the declared specification-space complexity of the lexical artifact under fixed evidence, rules and basis; it is distinct from the minimum distinguishing-test quantity $\sigma(P,\mu)$ in the program-specification papers. Disconnected solved components contribute their exhaustive witness counts and free coordinates contribute their remaining domain sizes. The marginal domain-log sum remains an upper bound, not a substitute for the joint count. Solver limits and missing certificates leave exact counts unavailable; conflicts earn no completion credit. This is a count of admissible representations, without an assumed distribution over their semantic correctness.

The first whole-corpus measurement reads active sparse release 021 without changing it. The fixed cohort contains **401,595 lexical definitions over 67,173 normalized spellings**, plus one separately reported sentence construction. It has **410,970 filled banks**, including **280,858 explicit zeros**, and **22,078,350 unresolved banks**. Every lexical definition has complete joint-search receipts. The corpus joint log-space is **32,819,443.22562693 bits**, compared with the marginal-domain upper bound **34,993,356.827796936 bits**. Thus the existing constraints already retain approximately **2,173,913.60217 bits** of coupled exclusions invisible to a marginal-domain-only report. This difference is existing structure, not newly learned information in this phase. Mean unresolved banks per definition are **54.97665558585142**; the spelling-weighted mean of per-sense averages is **54.79586979239802**.

